from assessment_writer import write_review

def compact(sid,title,rows,appraisal):
    write_review(sid,title,[(a,b.split(' | '),c) for a,b,c in rows],appraisal)

compact('MF-SCHIZOPHRENIA-PARENT-2023','Schizophrenia parent trial: preliminary cognitive benefit, not established functional recovery',[
('Objectives; Methods; NCT03271866','Test adjunctive metformin for cognitive impairment in schizophrenia. | Current registry lists MCCB at 12 weeks; paper follows participants for 24 weeks. | Cognitive test performance and its relationship to metabolic and brain-connectivity changes.','The longer follow-up is not silently treated as the registered primary timepoint.'),
('Methods; Results; Fig 1','Clinically stable adults aged 18–65, predominantly young, with schizophrenia. | Second Xiangya Hospital, China; illness at most 5 years, at least 10% antipsychotic-associated weight gain; diabetes and major physical illness excluded. | 72 randomized, 48 metformin and 24 control; 59 completed 24 weeks, 39 and 20 respectively.','The completion population differs from the randomized population.'),
('Randomized study','Lilinghengtai metformin tablets. | Oral titration from 500 mg evening to 1500 mg/day in three doses by day 5, or maximum tolerated dose. | Fixed baseline antipsychotic treatment in both groups. | Assessments baseline, 12 and 24 weeks.','This is adjunctive treatment, not replacement of antipsychotics.'),
('Randomized study','Antipsychotics alone, without placebo. | 2:1 assignment. | Computer-generated random numbers. | Open-label treatment; independent cognitive evaluator blinded. | September 2017–April 2021.','Participant and clinician expectations may affect performance despite evaluator blinding.'),
('Procedures; MCCB evaluation; NCT03271866','Cognition primary; metabolic, symptom and imaging analyses additional. | Chinese MCCB composite and seven domains; PANSS, depression scale and resting-state fMRI. | 12 and 24 weeks. | Standardized cognitive test, not direct daily-function or quality-of-life improvement. | Post hoc responder threshold at least 5 MCCB points; functional clinical importance was not independently established.','A score gain is not proof of better everyday function.'),
('Results Par21; Fig 2; Table S2 reference','Adjusted 24-week composite advantage 3.43 points for metformin. | Post hoc responders 28/39 versus 6/20 completers. | Composite 95% CI 1.83–5.02 points; responder OR 5.94, CI 1.82–19.40. | Between-arm cognitive advantage; domain differences also reported, including processing speed 3.75 and verbal learning 5.71 points. | Responder interval is wide; no equivalently verified functional outcome estimate.','Do not substitute within-arm metabolic improvement for the between-arm cognitive effect.'),
('Methods Par10,13,17,19; Results Par20','13 of 72 did not complete 24 weeks. | Linear mixed models accommodate missing follow-up under assumptions; eight lacked week-24 MRI because of COVID restrictions. | Pill returns; reported mean adherence 96.1%, all over 80%. | Low baseline cognitive-deficit participants excluded from imaging/correlation analyses; endpoint-specific samples differ. | Cognition mixed models; Table 1 metabolic analysis labels 40 and 23, despite 48 and 24 randomized. | Participant is the experimental unit; brain voxels and visits are not independent patients.','The denominators require endpoint-specific reporting rather than a single trial-wide number.'),
('Procedures; Safety evaluation','Treatment-Emergent Symptom Scale at each visit. | Safety report explicitly uses 45 metformin recipients for decreased appetite. | Decreased appetite 15/45, 33.3%, versus 0% in control; other differences described as nonsignificant. | GAP: Complete withdrawal reasons and adverse-event Table S9 are not in the acquired main report. | 24 weeks.','Neither a nonsignificant comparison nor the authors\' general safety wording excludes uncommon harms.'),
('Methods; Discussion; current registry','Open-label small single-center study with attrition and selected imaging subsets. | Randomization helps baseline balance; expectations and post-randomization selection remain. | Registry 12-week primary versus emphasized 24-week report; full historical protocol comparison unavailable. | Numerous domains, correlations and mediation tests; imaging used cluster FWE correction but this does not correct every analysis. | Cognitive CI excludes zero, while small responder and imaging subsets remain imprecise. | Published denominators differ by cognitive, metabolic and safety analyses; do not harmonize by assumption.','Correlational mediation cannot establish a causal biological pathway.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Stable early-course schizophrenia with antipsychotic-associated weight gain. | Adjunctive oral 1500 mg/day, not all psychiatric populations or doses. | Cognition test performance, not symptom remission or independent living. | Does not establish benefit in diabetes, older adults, unstable psychosis or long-duration disease.','The broad term cognitive enhancer would overstate this evidence.'),
('NCT03271866; CP017 Methods','One parent randomized cohort. | CP017 is the 2025 exploratory TCA/hippocampal analysis of this same cohort. | 58 metabolite and 33 imaging participants in CP017 overlap this trial. | Current guide references reconciled; no exhaustive replication review.','Count one trial, not a parent and a new mechanistic RCT.'),
('Byline; acknowledgments; competing interests','Tiannan Shao, Jing Huang, Renrong Wu and colleagues. | Second Xiangya Hospital, Central South University. | National Natural Science Foundation of China 81622018 and 82072096. | Brand/manufacturer specified; donation not established. | Authors declare no competing interests.','Funding and disclosed interests do not replace design appraisal.'),
('Full XML; current registry','Published Translational Psychiatry 2023 report, DOI 10.1038/s41398-023-02616-x. | Full text acquired and assessed. | GAP: Separate full protocol and supplemental safety/flow tables not successfully acquired. | NCT03271866 current record inspected; first posted September 5, 2017, after listed August 28 start. | No correction or retraction identified in retrieved article/metadata. | November 3–4, 2026.','This remains a main-report assessment with explicit supplementary gaps.'),
('G-METFORMIN neurological chapter','Schizophrenia cognitive benefit and mechanism. | Supports a preliminary cognitive test signal, with important bias and functional-outcome limits. | Authors\' stronger efficacy language is narrowed editorially; brain changes are associations.','Use parent results for clinical estimates and CP017 only for exploratory context.'),
('Synthesis','Promising but unconfirmed adjunctive cognitive effect in a selected population. | Clinical importance, durability, real-life function and mechanism remain uncertain. | Larger placebo-controlled trials with prespecified cognition and functioning outcomes could change this judgment.','No independent clinical replication is supplied by CP017.')
], 'A positive cognitive test finding with substantial limits: open-label treatment, small sample, multiple analyses and incomplete functional assessment. The later mechanistic paper is a companion, not independent replication.')

compact('CP017','Schizophrenia TCA companion: exploratory associations in the existing trial',[
('Methods Par45; Discussion Par60','Explore TCA metabolites, hippocampal connectivity and cognitive change after adjunctive metformin. | Explicitly post hoc exploratory analysis. | A proposed biological explanation of an existing trial signal, not a fresh efficacy test.','Preserve exploratory status.'),
('Methods Par45–48; Table 1','Human schizophrenia participants from the parent cohort, median age 22. | Stable illness and antipsychotic treatment; selected available plasma and follow-up. | Parent 72; metabolite analysis 58, 40 metformin and 18 control; final MRI 33, 21 and 12.','Biomarker and imaging subsets are smaller than the randomized trial.'),
('Methods; Additional file 1 eligibility','Metformin tablets as in the parent report. | Up to 500 mg three times daily orally, or maximum tolerated dose. | Stable antipsychotics, doses converted to chlorpromazine equivalents. | Baseline, 12 and 24 weeks.','Use the parent for the product and titration history.'),
('Methods Par45','Antipsychotics alone. | Inherited 2:1 randomized parent allocation. | Computer random-number generator in parent. | Open-label treatment; trained evaluators, parent cognitive evaluator masked. | Samples collected in the September 2017–April 2021 parent trial.','Post hoc sample availability is not randomized.'),
('Methods Par46–50; supplement assay methods','Post hoc metabolites and imaging; cognition correlations exploratory. | LC-MS/MS for nine plasma metabolites, MCCB and 3-T resting-state fMRI. | Weeks 12 and 24. | Circulating biomarkers and functional connectivity, not validated surrogates for recovery. | NA: No validated clinical benefit threshold for these metabolite/connectivity changes.','Plasma concentrations do not directly measure neuronal TCA flux.'),
('Tables 2–3; Results Par56–58; supplement Tables S1–S2','At week 24 adjusted lactate means 194.34 versus 342.43 micrograms/mL; pyruvate 43.45 versus 81.62. | Metabolite analysis 40 versus 18; MRI 21 versus 12. | Lactate between-arm difference −148.09 micrograms/mL, CI −184.61 to −111.57; pyruvate −38.17, CI −47.18 to −29.17. | Abstract lactate −80.81 and pyruvate −17.51 are within-metformin changes, not between-arm effects. | Imaging contrasts −0.334 at week 12 and 0.284 at week 24; exploratory correlations lack multiplicity-adjusted confirmation.','This resolves the crucial within-arm versus between-arm distinction.'),
('Methods Par48–50','14 parent participants absent from plasma analysis; MRI selected 41 then excluded eight after preprocessing. | Last observation carried forward for missing TCA/cognition; sensitivity analysis on observed and imputed data; MRI not imputed. | Parent reports high pill adherence; no independent biomarker adherence confirmation. | Availability and image-quality exclusions; motion threshold 3 mm or 3 degrees in supplement. | Post hoc available-case subset of randomized cohort. | Participant, not metabolite, brain region, voxel or repeated sample.','Selection and imputation can distort association estimates.'),
('Parent safety report; companion scope','No new systematic harms analysis in this mechanistic report. | NA: Biomarker subset is not a newly exposed safety cohort. | Parent reports decreased appetite; companion cannot independently confirm safety. | NR: No new treatment-withdrawal analysis in CP017. | Same 24-week exposure window.','Absence of safety results here is not absence of adverse events.'),
('Methods Par50; Discussion Par67; supplement','Post hoc selection, small imaging sample, open-label parent and multiple correlational analyses. | Metabolic status, antipsychotic exposure and other factors may confound mediator-outcome associations. | Analysis not prospectively specified; current registry cognition primary does not prospectively validate these exploratory tests. | Bonferroni used for some metabolite contrasts and FWE for imaging; correlations/interactions deliberately use raw P values. | Large metabolite contrasts do not resolve precision of clinical benefit; MRI n=33. | Abstract emphasizes within-arm changes; Table 3 is the between-arm comparison.','Do not infer TCA activation causes cognitive improvement.'),
('Methods; Discussion','Young stable schizophrenia cohort with weight gain; not general cognitive decline. | Same adjunctive metformin regimen as parent. | Mechanistic hypothesis, not a treatment-response biomarker ready for clinical use. | Peripheral metabolites and selected imaging do not establish central metabolic flux or mediation.','This cannot support an independent Parkinson or dementia claim.'),
('NCT03271866; parent citation','Same trial family as MF-SCHIZOPHRENIA-PARENT-2023. | Follow-up metabolomic/imaging report. | All analyzed participants are drawn from the parent trial. | Bounded identity reconciliation, no comprehensive replication search.','Adds mechanistic observations, zero new randomized trials.'),
('Byline; Funding; declarations','Jingda Cai, Jingmei Xiao, Tiannan Shao, Hualin Cai, Renrong Wu and colleagues. | Second Xiangya Hospital and collaborating institutions. | Chinese national science grants, Hunan innovation support and institutional clinical specialty research support. | Assay standards purchased from specified suppliers; no inference of sponsor efficacy control. | Authors declare no competing interests.','The assay validation reports linearity, precision and recovery, not proof of clinical surrogacy.'),
('Full XML/PDF; Additional file 1; registry','Published BMC Medicine 2025, DOI 10.1186/s12916-025-04218-4. | Full report and supplemental DOCX acquired; assay, eligibility, missing-data and sensitivity tables inspected. | Additional file 1 includes LC-MS/MS calibration and imaging preprocessing. | NCT03271866 current registry and parent compared; full historical registry audit not performed. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved current source records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Independent reproduction of the raw-data analysis remains pending.'),
('G-METFORMIN mechanism and neurological chapters','Potential TCA/connectivity explanation of cognitive signal. | Indirect mechanistic support only; not independent efficacy evidence. | Observations are associations; author interpretation of activation is a hypothesis.','Use no clinical-benefit grade from these biomarkers alone.'),
('Synthesis','Exploratory biological changes merit further study but do not prove a treatment mechanism. | Causality, reproducibility and relation to everyday function remain unknown. | Preregistered replicated mediator analyses with clinical outcomes and direct metabolic measurements are needed.','Keep this report in the same family as the parent.')
], 'Substantive post hoc mechanistic assessment. The abstract within-arm changes must not be mislabeled treatment effects; Table 3 supplies the proper between-arm contrasts. Correlations do not establish mediation or add a second randomized trial.')

compact('CP018','Sarcopenia narrative review: historical context, superseded for later trial results',[
('Purpose; abstract','Review diabetes drugs as potential sarcopenia therapies. | Narrative review; no registered systematic-review protocol reported. | Biological rationale and research priorities.','Not a randomized treatment study.'),
('Scope','Mixed cells, animals and human populations, many with type 2 diabetes. | Heterogeneous settings and definitions of muscle health. | NA: No newly recruited or pooled participant population.','Do not sum the review\'s study citations as independent trial participants.'),
('Metformin section','Multiple diabetes drug classes, including metformin. | Different doses/routes across cited studies; no review-wide regimen. | Exercise and other interventions vary. | Durations differ by cited source.','No universal schedule can be extracted from this review.'),
('Evidence from observational and intervention studies','Various placebo, usual-care and observational comparators. | NA: No new allocation. | NA: Randomization applies to individual cited trials only. | NA: No participant-level blinding in a narrative review. | Literature available before publication in 2023.','The review itself cannot remove bias from its sources.'),
('Scope; conclusions','No primary efficacy endpoint for the review. | Muscle mass, strength, physical performance and proposed pathways. | Varies by underlying study. | Mix of clinical function and surrogates. | NA: No single meaningful-change threshold across this heterogeneous evidence.','Muscle mass and physical function are distinct outcomes.'),
('Metformin evidence Par30–32','Describes a positive small prefrailty study and blunted training adaptation in MASTERS. | NA: No pooled numerator or denominator. | NA: No meta-analytic estimate. | Direction and context vary; no unified metformin benefit estimate. | No review-level confidence interval or formal certainty analysis reported.','For guide numbers, use the acquired primary reports rather than copying secondary summaries.'),
('Review methods and evidence scope','NA: No participant attrition in the review itself. | NR: No complete reproducible search or missing-study analysis. | NA: Adherence belongs to underlying trials. | NR: Systematic inclusion/exclusion and duplicate-screening process not reported. | Narrative selected literature. | Individual source study, with potential overlap.','Unreported search methods limit confidence that all contradictory studies were included.'),
('Metformin and conclusions','Discusses gastrointestinal intolerance, lactic acidosis and class-specific risks. | NA: No review-wide exposure denominator. | Qualitative safety discussion, not a new comparative safety estimate. | NA: No pooled treatment withdrawal count. | Variable source durations.','Current labels and directly assessed trials take precedence for guide safety facts.'),
('Scope; conclusion','Narrative-selection and citation bias possible; heterogeneous designs. | Observational diabetes-drug comparisons confounded by indication and health status. | No prespecified systematic outcome hierarchy or formal bias assessment. | NA: No new inferential tests; selective emphasis across many studies remains possible. | No pooled uncertainty estimate. | Describes MET-PREVENT as underway; its 2025 results are now available.','The forward-looking statement is historical, not a current missing trial.'),
('Conclusions','Research rationale for people with or at risk of sarcopenia. | Does not identify a proven metformin regimen for those without diabetes. | Does not establish benefit on falls, independence or survival. | Diabetes observations cannot be transferred directly to frail adults without diabetes.','Use as context only.'),
('References; current guide inventory','Secondary review, no new study family. | MASTERS is separately assessed; MET-PREVENT later reports results. | Underlying trials must not be counted again through this review. | Narrative scope, not an exhaustive updated search.','Source count is not independent-study count.'),
('Byline; declarations','Miles Witham, Avan Sayer and colleagues. | Geriatrics and muscle-research collaborators. | No specific review funding; several authors acknowledge NIHR Newcastle Biomedical Research Centre support. | NA: No trial products supplied for this review. | Witham and Sayer disclose being MET-PREVENT investigators.','Retain that relevant disclosure without treating it as proof of bias.'),
('Full XML/PDF','Published Drugs & Aging 2023, DOI 10.1007/s40266-023-01042-4. | Full article acquired and relevant metformin sections assessed. | No necessary separate meta-analysis dataset identified. | NA: No registered systematic-review protocol reported. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records; time-sensitive trial-status statements superseded. | November 3–4, 2026.','This assessment is limited to its use as metformin background, not every drug class discussed.'),
('G-METFORMIN physical-function chapter','Why diabetes drugs were investigated for sarcopenia. | Supports rationale and uncertainty, not demonstrated treatment efficacy. | Authors call for trials; editorial synthesis includes later negative MET-PREVENT results.','A historical review must not outrank a later directly relevant RCT.'),
('Synthesis','Mechanistic promise justified trials but was not proof of improved function. | Optimal population and net effect remained unknown at publication. | Later randomized functional outcomes should update the synthesis.','MET-PREVENT now provides such evidence and is assessed separately.')
], 'A useful narrative account of rationale and conflicting early evidence. It is neither a systematic efficacy synthesis nor an additional trial, and its description of MET-PREVENT as ongoing is superseded.')
